docs/architecture/types-and-output-contract.md:83:`PropType` and `CollectedType` do **not** derive `Serialize`/`Deserialize`. `CollectedType` implements both by hand; `PropType` implements only `Serialize`, since nothing reads the canonical JSON back in Rust. `CollectedType` (`crates/core/src/types/collected.rs`) round-trips through a private `to_json_value`/`from_json_value` pair that builds and reads a `serde_json::Value`. `PropType` (`crates/core/src/types/output.rs`) streams its `Serialize` impl straight into the serializer as a map with `kind` first and the fields in a fixed order (an `OpaqueReason` is written by the private `WireReason` as `{ type, ... }`).
docs/architecture/types-and-output-contract.md:104:`OpaqueReason` derives normally. It isn't recursive — every variant's payload is a `String` or a unit — so it never hit the recursion-limit problem the ADR is about. The _serialization of an `OpaqueReason` value_ is still hand-assembled by `WireReason` (`types/output.rs`, matching each reason to its own `{ type, ... }` shape) when `PropType`'s `Serialize` writes the `Opaque` variant, and hand-parsed in `PropType::from_tagged_value` — but that's because it's embedded inside `PropType`'s own manual impl, not because `OpaqueReason` itself needs one. This is a small, real documentation bug in ADR-0002 worth a one-line fix, independent of anything else in this file.
